chapbook — third-party notices
==============================

chapbook itself is licensed under either of Apache License 2.0
(LICENSE-APACHE) or the MIT license (LICENSE-MIT), at your option.

This file is about the *other* code a chapbook binary contains. A source
checkout of this repository contains none of it — the crates below are
fetched from crates.io at build time — so this matters when you distribute
a built binary, not when you clone the repo.

The list is the Linux dependency graph with every feature enabled, which is
the largest a chapbook build gets: 414 third-party crates, of which 355 are
plain MIT, plain Apache-2.0, or offer a choice including one of them. The
remaining 59 are named below, because each carries a term the MIT/Apache
pair does not.

Nothing here conflicts with releasing chapbook under MIT OR Apache-2.0.
There is no GPL-, AGPL-, or LGPL-only dependency in the graph.

Regenerating this list, and the caveat
--------------------------------------

    cargo deny check licenses          # the CI gate; policy in deny.toml
    cargo tree -e normal --target x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu \
        --prefix none --format '{l}|{p}' | sort -u

This file is a human-scale summary, not a substitute for shipping license
texts. MIT and the BSD licenses require the copyright notice and permission
text to travel with a binary, and reproducing 414 of those here would go
stale on the first `cargo update`. Generate the full bundle at release time:

    cargo about generate about.hbs > THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES.html


